Z||000006||Oreif||06-18-2000||07:54 AM||||For the past 15 years Los Angeles use to have the highest average price for gas. As of last week LA's average was $1.82 per gallon. Now the midwest is the highest!<BR>Last week the average for Chicago/Milwaukee was $2.02! In fact the national average was only $1.66 per gallon! (looks like price gouging to me!)<P>As for the original topic, You could also have a bad sending unit in the tank.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-18-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000007||Homicidal||06-18-2000||10:42 AM||||Can I run a test on any of these suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||06-18-2000||01:33 PM||||Use your trip gauge (if it works) or your odometer, fill up your car and write down the mileage, write down the gas gauge readings at say 50 and 100 miles then at about 150 miles write it down and fill up the tank again.  Depending on your engine and how you drive you should be down about 5-8 gallons.<P>Since you say the gauge is acting funny and not that you're getting horrible mileage there's not much risk that you'd run out of gas within 150 miles.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||jlruiz67||06-19-2000||11:22 AM||||Hello<BR>I had that problem a while ago and it turned out to be the float that is attached to the fuel pump inside the gas tank was sticking. The bad part is that to find out you must drop the gas tank. The good news is that it is a easy job if you have jack stands. Also you can change your fuel pump once is out, as preventive maintenance. If it turns out that it wasn't the float you can confort yourself in knowing that you have a new fuel pump in there.<BR>||||reg||4
